#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088678} :activity/id #uuid "b3fd6ef1-04ad-5c0a-b327-86d38cf19448" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049763 :topic/id #uuid "11d9e0d4-4303-597d-9364-282253c19d56" :topic/math-academy-id 886} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088853 :tutorial/content "Consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos \\sqrt{x} \\, \\textrm dx\\,\n$$\n\nUnfortunately, we cannot compute this integral using any of our standard methods. However, we *can* express this integral as an infinite series if we first write the integrand $\\cos \\sqrt{x}$ as a Maclaurin series.\n\nLet's start with the Maclaurin series expansion of $\\cos{x},$ valid for all $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}\t=1 - \\frac{1}{2!}x^2 + \\frac{1}{4!}x^4+\\cdots \\,.\n$$\n\n    \n\nReplacing $x$ with $\\sqrt{x}$ in the above equation, we find the series expansion for $\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x} & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}(\\sqrt{x})^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}(\\sqrt{x})^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nIntegrating both sides of this equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}\\,dx & =\\int (1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+⋯)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+⋯ \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{72}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nAnd we're done! We've just found the Maclaurin series expression for $\\displaystyle \\int\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$\n\nWe can apply the same method to any function $f(x)$ having a power series expansion of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=a_0+a_1 x+a_2 x^2 + \\cdots\\,.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({a}_{0}+{a}_{1}x+{a}_{2}{x}^{2}+⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={a}_{0}\\int \\,\\text{d}x+{a}_{1}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+{a}_{2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+⋯ \\\\  & =C+{a}_{0}x+{a}_{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{a}_{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is valid for the same radius of convergence as the series expansion of $f(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b0a3a223-98fa-56e3-b1df-6cb6d1bcdaf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1322 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6ceb1bdb-03e5-5b90-9089-ebb348602688" :step/math-academy-id 2600 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087639 :question/id #uuid "16b3f250-dfcb-5fa6-bfdb-4acf92d8bd37" :question/math-academy-id "e-765"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea68c90-99a8-5254-b799-5f0ed51615a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532725}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Indefinite Integrals Using Taylor Series"} :step/id #uuid "3701ede4-42d5-5b06-ae18-a15fcdbae21e" :step/math-academy-id 2601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087640 :question/id #uuid "4211b707-bff1-5855-9449-16e14858e6ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-766"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f133583-cb11-5007-bdef-641838aff5e4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532664} {:db/id #edb/re